# Environment-dependent: requires journal access and systemd-journald.service.
#
# Read at most 80 records from the last 15 minutes. yesh always disables the
# pager, uses machine-readable JSON Lines, caps output at 4 MiB, validates
# every record, and treats stderr diagnostics as failure. Empty output is a
# successful query: it means no matching records were available in that
# interval, not that the journal was silently ignored.
#
# There is no `follow`: this is a finite report suitable for CI and cron.
# The exact unit is required; journalctl patterns and multiple units are not
# accepted by this typed operation.
#
# `--unit` has systemd's unit semantics: a slice query includes records from
# child units. This example selects one concrete service instead. Access
# denial, an unavailable journal/bus, stderr diagnostics or malformed JSON are
# errors; only an empty stdout result is the normal "no matching records"
# result.
#
# Bash equivalent (with easy-to-miss safety details):
#   journalctl --no-pager --output=json --unit systemd-journald.service \
#     --since=-15m --lines=80
# yesh makes the bounds part of the statement and its plan.

# Role of yesh: keep the service or journal request bounded and visible; systemd remains the source of state.
journal systemd-journald.service since 15m lines 80
